<p>MOV and MP4 are both <strong>containers</strong>: wrappers that hold the video, the sound and some information about them. The picture quality depends on what is inside, not on the wrapper.</p>

<h2>Where they come from</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>MOV</strong> is Apple's QuickTime format. iPhones and Macs save video as MOV.</li>
  <li><strong>MP4</strong> is an international standard that was based on the QuickTime format. It is the format websites, Windows, Android, TVs and most apps expect.</li>
</ul>
<p>Because MP4 grew out of MOV, the two are very similar inside. That is why converting between them is often quick.</p>

<h2>What matters more: the video format inside</h2>
<p>The thing that decides whether a video plays, and how big it is, is the video format (codec) inside the container:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>H.264</strong> is the most widely supported. Almost every phone, computer, browser and app can play it.</li>
  <li><strong>HEVC (H.265)</strong> is newer and makes smaller files at the same quality. iPhones record in it by default. Some Windows PCs need an extra add-on to play it, and some websites do not accept it.</li>
</ul>
<p>So "my MOV will not play" is usually an HEVC problem, not a MOV problem.</p>

<h2>Which to use</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sharing, uploading or sending to someone on Windows or Android:</strong> MP4 with H.264. It plays nearly everywhere.</li>
  <li><strong>Keeping videos on Apple devices:</strong> MOV is fine.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Does converting lose quality?</h2>
<p>It depends on what is inside:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A MOV with H.264 video can be repackaged as MP4 without re-encoding. That is quick and loses nothing.</li>
  <li>A MOV with HEVC video has to be re-encoded to H.264 to play everywhere. That takes longer, and the file can get bigger, because H.264 needs more data for the same picture.</li>
</ul>
<p>The converter tells you which of the two will happen before it starts.</p>
